WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.940
How do you test what a single gene does inside a living animal?

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.400
Cells in a dish give a hint, yet a dish is not an embryo.

3
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:12.840
To test one gene in a whole body, add it to an embryo.

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.300
That added DNA is a transgene.

5
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:24.120
First, pick what to add: a gene, a regulatory switch, or a visible label.

6
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:30.440
Next, pronuclear injection delivers that DNA into a fertilized egg.

7
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:36.820
When it joins the germ line, the cells that pass DNA on, offspring can inherit it.

8
00:00:37.783 --> 00:00:42.323
As the transgenic embryo grows, cells read the transgene in two ways.

9
00:00:43.563 --> 00:00:48.403
One tests extra or ectopic expression, turning a gene on where it normally stays

10
00:00:48.403 --> 00:00:54.283
off. Another builds a reporter line, giving specific cells a visible tag.

11
00:00:55.663 --> 00:01:00.083
Adding a gene to an embryo tests what it can do inside a living animal.

12
00:01:01.183 --> 00:01:02.683
Now pause and predict.

13
00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:08.243
Suppose a reporter mouse has neurons that glow when calcium rises.

14
00:01:09.623 --> 00:01:12.643
Each time the mouse turns left, those neurons light up.

15
00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:18.203
Does that glowing signal prove that firing those neurons turns the mouse left?

16
00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:28.047
No. A reporter is a voltmeter, not a switch.

17
00:01:29.467 --> 00:01:32.327
Watching neurons glow cannot prove they cause the turn.

18
00:01:33.547 --> 00:01:38.307
To test cause, drive them yourself with a light-triggered actuator or an electrode.

19
00:01:39.627 --> 00:01:41.047
Then watch what the mouse does.

20
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.080
Two more limits keep transgenic results honest.

21
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.320
Adding a gene tests what is enough, not what is needed.

22
00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.480
For necessity, disrupt the gene in a knockout mouse.

23
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.860
Also, forcing an effect cannot prove nature used that route, so compare two versions

24
00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:00.900
of the same DNA.

25
00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:05.993
With those guards, transgenic mice unlocked major discoveries.

26
00:02:07.433 --> 00:02:14.293
In 1991, Koopman and colleagues added a 14-kilobase fragment containing Sry to

27
00:02:14.293 --> 00:02:16.313
chromosomally female mouse embryos.

28
00:02:17.853 --> 00:02:22.713
Even with no Y chromosome, that single gene was sufficient to make them develop

29
00:02:22.713 --> 00:02:23.713
testes.

30
00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:31.207
In 2002, Anjen Chenn and Christopher Walsh put stabilized beta β-catenin in neural

31
00:02:31.207 --> 00:02:33.207
progenitors of transgenic mice.

32
00:02:34.667 --> 00:02:38.807
Stabilized means the cell cannot break it down, so its signal keeps going.

33
00:02:40.027 --> 00:02:45.087
Those brain-building cells kept dividing, and the smooth cortex buckled into folds.

34
00:02:46.427 --> 00:02:50.267
The mice did not live past birth, and their folding was unlike primates.

35
00:02:51.167 --> 00:02:55.827
So extra divisions can enlarge a cortex, not how primates got theirs.

36
00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:02.990
In 2012, Youngjin Lee, Jeffrey Rothstein and colleagues used reporter mice to label

37
00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:09.990
MCT1. The visible tag showed this transporter is concentrated in oligodendrocytes,

38
00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:11.950
glia that wrap axons.

39
00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.270
They then lowered it and watched the neurons.

40
00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:22.190
Labeling cells or driving traits, one added gene tests biology inside a living body.
